/*
    UILoadingScreen.uss
    Layout for the full-screen loading overlay. Colours come from FishMMO-Theme.uss.

    The bar used to be a 10px strip pinned to the very bottom edge of the screen, full width and
    with no track behind it — at 1080p that is under 1% of the screen height, in the corner of
    the player's vision, against whatever the artwork happened to be. It was easy to miss and
    impossible to read a value from.

    It is now a centred block low on the screen: status line, a bar with a real track and a
    percentage on it, and a hint line. Nothing else — the artwork is the point of a loading
    screen and anything laid over it competes with it. An earlier revision added a brand banner
    and a scrim across the lower half; both were removed. The banner duplicated the launcher's
    and read as clutter, and the scrim put a hard horizontal seam across the middle of the art
    that looked like a rendering fault rather than a design.

    Legibility comes from the elements themselves instead: the bar has its own dark track, and
    the text over it carries a shadow. That works on any artwork without touching the artwork.
*/

.loading-screen {
    flex-grow: 1;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: flex-end;
}

/* Full-bleed artwork, assigned from C#. */
.loading-image {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    -unity-background-scale-mode: scale-and-crop;
}

/* ── The loading block ──────────────────────────────────── */

.loading-panel {
    width: 46%;
    min-width: 380px;
    max-width: 720px;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: 8%;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/*
    Status line for waits the player cannot see the reason for — currently the reconnect
    backoff, which can hold the overlay for minutes while nothing visibly happens. Hidden
    (display: none) from C# whenever the text is empty, so an ordinary scene load shows only
    the bar.
*/
.loading-status {
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    white-space: normal;
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--text-bright);
    text-shadow: 0 2px 4px var(--shadow-text-hard);
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/*
    A real track, tall enough to read a fill against, and dark enough to carry the percentage
    without needing anything behind it.
*/
.loading-progress {
    width: 100%;
    height: 18px;
    position: relative;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.loading-progress__fill {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 0;
}

/* Percentage centred on the bar, so the number and the fill are read together. */
.loading-progress__text {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    font-size: 11px;
}

/* Destination scene name. Shadowed for the same reason the status line is. */
.loading-hint {
    margin-top: 10px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    white-space: normal;
    color: var(--abyss-100);
    text-shadow: 0 2px 4px var(--shadow-text-hard);
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/*
    ── The escape hatch ────────────────────────────────────────

    The overlay used to have no button of any kind, and it is raised by four independent drivers
    of which two (world entry and the armed reconnect) are cleared only by Hide(). Anything that
    left one of those set held the screen indefinitely with nothing on it to press: the loading
    overlay covers every other panel, so this was Alt+F4 territory.

    Collapsed rather than merely transparent so it takes no vertical space at all while hidden —
    the loading block is centred on itself, and an empty row would shift the bar off-centre on
    every ordinary scene load. Revealed from C# only after the overlay has outlasted any honest
    transition; see UITKLoadingScreen.
*/
.loading-actions {
    display: none;
    margin-top: 16px;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/*
    Says why the button has appeared. Carries .loading-status as well, which is where the colour
    and the shadow come from — repeating them here would mean a second colour literal in a panel
    stylesheet, and the palette is FishMMO-Theme.uss's job.
*/
.loading-stuck {
    font-size: 13px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
}

.loading-return-btn {
    min-width: 180px;
}
